From de6dde9ab1d83b0f529c88f7ebf89785a2b72742 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Raul Moran <moranguerra18@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 04:56:23 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] SlaveTimeSource: optionally run on the master's thread

A slave time source hands every grant from its master to its sinks on a
dispatcher thread of its own: per quantum, the master wakes the slave's
thread, the slave wakes its sinks' threads, and the reports come back the
same way. With N slaves (a machine each, for machines whose clocks must
follow their own CPUs) that is two hand-offs a quantum per machine and
2N + 1 threads that spin while they wait; on a 4-core host, three
machines' seven threads oversubscribe it, and the slaves' threads were a
quarter of the samples of a three-machine emulation.

RunsOnMasterThread, set before the slave is attached, removes the thread:
TimeSourceBase.InnerExecute is split into InnerExecuteBegin (up to granting
time to, or unblocking, every handle) and InnerExecuteEnd (waiting for
them, the sync phase), and the master, when it grants a slave time (or
unblocks it), begins the slave's iteration on its own thread, and ends it
when it waits for that slave's handle. The slave's sinks run beside the
master's other sinks as before; the slave's sync phase runs on the master's
thread, with the slave's thread time stamp (TimeDomainsManager
SwapCurrentThread). TimeHandle.RequestTimeInterval gains noWait for a sink
that cannot wait for its own source: it does not wait for a grant and,
when blocking, unblocks in place.

Off by default; when off, nothing changes. The slave time source tests run
both ways.
